# Relations are explicit predicates: the checker evaluates all four alternatives.
def relation(family,d,definitions,values,opts,predicates,answer,explanation,tip):
 q=new('numeric_comparison','nongeometric_comparison',family,d,'Compare the quantities. Which statement is true?\n'+definitions,opts,answer,explanation,tip,{'kind':'relations','values':values,'predicates':predicates},answerFormat='relationship')
 return q
relation('fraction-percent-equality',2,'P = 3/8 of 80; Q = 35% of 80; R = 0.3 × 100.',{'P':'3/8*80','Q':'35/100*80','R':'3/10*100'},['P = Q','Q > R','P = R > Q','P < Q < R'],['P==Q','Q>R','P==R and R>Q','P<Q and Q<R'],'P = R > Q','P = 30, Q = 28, and R = 30. Therefore P and R are equal and both exceed Q.','Find a common quantity before comparing different representations.')
relation('negative-order',2,'P = −3 × 4; Q = 5 − 14; R = −2 × 5.',{'P':'-3*4','Q':'5-14','R':'-2*5'},['P > Q > R','Q > R > P','R > P > Q','P = R'],['P>Q and Q>R','Q>R and R>P','R>P and P>Q','P==R'],'Q > R > P','P = −12, Q = −9, and R = −10. Among negatives, the number closer to zero is greater.','Place negative values on a mental number line.')
relation('operation-grouping',2,'P = 4 × (9 − 2); Q = 4 × 9 − 2; R = 4 × 9 − 4 × 2.',{'P':'4*(9-2)','Q':'4*9-2','R':'4*9-4*2'},['P = Q = R','P = R < Q','P > R','Q < R'],['P==Q and Q==R','P==R and R<Q','P>R','Q<R'],'P = R < Q','P and R both equal 28 by the distributive property. Q equals 34 because only 2 is subtracted.','Distribute a multiplier to every term inside parentheses.')
relation('fraction-near-one',2,'P = 11/12; Q = 7/8; R = 5/6.',{'P':'11/12','Q':'7/8','R':'5/6'},['P > Q > R','Q > P > R','R > Q > P','P = Q'],['P>Q and Q>R','Q>P and P>R','R>Q and Q>P','P==Q'],'P > Q > R','Their gaps below 1 are 1/12, 1/8, and 1/6. The smallest gap belongs to the greatest fraction.','Compare distances below 1 rather than dividing all three fractions.')
relation('ratio-decimal-order',2,'P = 7 ÷ 20; Q = 0.305; R = 33%.',{'P':'7/20','Q':'305/1000','R':'33/100'},['Q > P','P < R','P > R > Q','P = R'],['Q>P','P<R','P>R and R>Q','P==R'],'P > R > Q','P is 0.35, R is 0.33, and Q is 0.305. Thus P > R > Q.','Write each value to the same decimal precision.')
relation('two-quantity-sum',2,'P = 2³; Q = 3²; R = 4².',{'P':'2**3','Q':'3**2','R':'4**2'},['P + Q = R','P + Q > R','P > Q','R < Q'],['P+Q==R','P+Q>R','P>Q','R<Q'],'P + Q > R','P = 8, Q = 9, and R = 16. Their sum P + Q is 17, which is greater than 16.','Evaluate the requested relationship, not only the largest single quantity.')
relation('percentage-complement',2,'P = 60% of 45; Q = 40% of 45; R = 9.',{'P':'60/100*45','Q':'40/100*45','R':'9'},['P − Q = R','P + R = Q','Q = R','P = Q'],['P-Q==R','P+R==Q','Q==R','P==Q'],'P − Q = R','P is 27 and Q is 18. Their difference is 9, equal to R.','The difference between 60% and 40% is 20% of the same base.')
relation('reciprocal-size',2,'P = 1 ÷ 0.25; Q = 1 ÷ 0.5; R = 1 ÷ 2.',{'P':'1/(1/4)','Q':'1/(1/2)','R':'1/2'},['P < Q < R','P = Q','P > Q > R','R > P'],['P<Q and Q<R','P==Q','P>Q and Q>R','R>P'],'P > Q > R','P = 4, Q = 2, and R = 1/2. Dividing by a smaller positive number gives a larger result.','Division by a fraction below 1 increases a positive number.')
relation('mean-versus-midpoint',2,'P = the average of 8 and 20; Q = the average of 11 and 17; R = 15.',{'P':'(8+20)/2','Q':'(11+17)/2','R':'15'},['P < Q','P = Q < R','Q > R','P = R'],['P<Q','P==Q and Q<R','Q>R','P==R'],'P = Q < R','Both pairs total 28, so both averages are 14. Each is less than 15.','Pairs with the same sum have the same average.')
relation('equal-rate-different-whole',2,'P = 3/5 of 50; Q = 3/5 of 45; R = 2/3 of 45.',{'P':'3/5*50','Q':'3/5*45','R':'2/3*45'},['P = Q','P = R > Q','Q > R','P < R'],['P==Q','P==R and R>Q','Q>R','P<R'],'P = R > Q','P = 30, Q = 27, and R = 30. P and R are equal; both exceed Q.','The same fraction of different wholes does not give the same amount.')
relation('decimal-product-size',2,'P = 0.8 × 0.6; Q = 0.8 + 0.6; R = 0.8 − 0.6.',{'P':'8/10*6/10','Q':'8/10+6/10','R':'8/10-6/10'},['P > Q','R > P','Q > P > R','P = R'],['P>Q','R>P','Q>P and P>R','P==R'],'Q > P > R','P = 0.48, Q = 1.4, and R = 0.2. So Q > P > R.','Multiplying two positive decimals below 1 makes a smaller product.')
relation('fractional-sums',3,'P = 1/2 + 1/3; Q = 3/4; R = 5/6.',{'P':'1/2+1/3','Q':'3/4','R':'5/6'},['P = R > Q','P < Q','P > R','Q = R'],['P==R and R>Q','P<Q','P>R','Q==R'],'P = R > Q','P = 3/6 + 2/6 = 5/6, equal to R. In twelfths, 5/6 is 10/12 and 3/4 is 9/12.','Simplify one expression before comparing it with the others.')
relation('percent-change-same-delta',3,'P = the increase from 50 to 60 as a fraction of 50; Q = the decrease from 60 to 50 as a fraction of 60; R = 1/5.',{'P':'(60-50)/50','Q':'(60-50)/60','R':'1/5'},['P = Q','Q > R','P = R > Q','P < Q'],['P==Q','Q>R','P==R and R>Q','P<Q'],'P = R > Q','P is 10/50 = 1/5. Q is 10/60 = 1/6. Equal changes divided by different starting values give different rates.','Always identify the base used in each fraction.')
relation('factor-comparison',1,'P = 12 × 7; Q = 6 × 14; R = 3 × 28.',{'P':'12*7','Q':'6*14','R':'3*28'},['P > Q','R < Q','P = Q = R','Q > R'],['P>Q','R<Q','P==Q and Q==R','Q>R'],'P = Q = R','Each product is 84. Halving one factor while doubling the other leaves the product unchanged.','Use factor compensation instead of multiplying three times.')
relation('absolute-versus-signed',2,'P = the distance from −7 to 0; Q = −7; R = the distance from 3 to −3.',{'P':'7','Q':'-7','R':'3-(-3)'},['P = Q','Q > R','R > P','P > R > Q'],['P==Q','Q>R','R>P','P>R and R>Q'],'P > R > Q','Distances are nonnegative: P = 7 and R = 6. Q is −7, so P > R > Q.','Distance is a length, not a signed coordinate.')
relation('square-versus-product',2,'P = 15²; Q = 14 × 16; R = 13 × 17.',{'P':'15**2','Q':'14*16','R':'13*17'},['P > Q > R','R > Q > P','P = Q','Q < R'],['P>Q and Q>R','R>Q and Q>P','P==Q','Q<R'],'P > Q > R','P = 225, Q = 224, and R = 221. The products decrease as the factors move equally away from 15.','Use (a − b)(a + b) = a² − b² to compare efficiently.')
relation('fraction-of-difference',2,'P = 1/2 of (30 − 12); Q = 1/2 of 30, minus 12; R = 3.',{'P':'(30-12)/2','Q':'30/2-12','R':'3'},['P = Q','P > Q = R','P < R','Q > R'],['P==Q','P>Q and Q==R','P<R','Q>R'],'P > Q = R','P = 18/2 = 9. Q = 15 − 12 = 3, equal to R. The parentheses change what is halved.','Track which part of the expression the fraction applies to.')
relation('fraction-order-signs',3,'P = −2/3; Q = −3/4; R = −5/8.',{'P':'-2/3','Q':'-3/4','R':'-5/8'},['Q > P > R','P > R > Q','R > P > Q','P = Q'],['Q>P and P>R','P>R and R>Q','R>P and P>Q','P==Q'],'R > P > Q','In twenty-fourths, P = −16/24, Q = −18/24, and R = −15/24. The greatest is the one closest to zero.','Use a common denominator, then remember that negative order reverses size intuition.')
