visual('bar-missing-total',2,'The chart shows books read from Monday through Thursday. How many books must be read on Friday to make the five-day total 30?','4|5|6|9','6','The four bars total 5 + 9 + 7 + 3 = 24. Friday must add 30 − 24 = 6 books.','Find the total already shown before finding the missing part.',{'type':'bars','labels':['Mon','Tue','Wed','Thu'],'values':[5,9,7,3],'maximum':10,'tick':1,'unit':'Books'}, {'kind':'numeric','expression':'30-(5+9+7+3)','visualCheck':'barTotal'})
visual('bar-average',2,'The bars show the number of points earned in three rounds. How many points above the three-round average is Round B?','1|2|3|4','2','The total is 4 + 10 + 10 = 24, so the average is 8. Round B has 10 points, which is 2 above average.','An average uses every round, including tied values.',{'type':'bars','labels':['A','B','C'],'values':[4,10,10],'maximum':12,'tick':2,'unit':'Points'}, {'kind':'numeric','expression':'10-(4+10+10)/3','visualCheck':'barMean'})
visual('bar-transfer',3,'Two counters are moved from group Q to group R. After the move, which relationship is true?',['P > Q > R','Q > P > R','Q = P','R > Q'],'Q > P > R','The original counts are P = 8, Q = 12, R = 4. After moving two, Q = 10 and R = 6; P stays 8. So Q > P > R.','Update both the giving and receiving group, but leave the third group unchanged.',{'type':'bars','labels':['P','Q','R'],'values':[8,12,4],'maximum':12,'tick':2,'unit':'Counters'}, {'kind':'relations','values':{'P':'8','Q':'12-2','R':'4+2'},'predicates':['P>Q and Q>R','Q>P and P>R','Q==P','R>Q'],'visualCheck':'barTransfer'})
visual('equal-perimeter-distinct-area',2,'P is a rectangle and Q is a square with the dimensions shown. Which statement is true?',['P has a larger perimeter than Q','They have equal perimeters, but Q has the larger area','They have equal areas','Q has a smaller area than P'],'They have equal perimeters, but Q has the larger area','P has perimeter 2(7 + 3) = 20 and area 21. Q has perimeter 4 × 5 = 20 and area 25. The perimeters match, but Q has more area.','Perimeter and area measure different things; compare them separately.',{'type':'rectangles','panels':[{'label':'P','width':7,'height':3},{'label':'Q','width':5,'height':5}],'unit':'cm'}, {'kind':'relations','values':{'PP':'2*(7+3)','QP':'4*5','PA':'7*3','QA':'5*5'},'predicates':['PP>QP','PP==QP and QA>PA','PA==QA','QA<PA'],'visualCheck':'rectangles'})
visual('rectangle-perimeter-gap',2,'Both figures are rectangles. How many centimetres greater is the perimeter of T than the perimeter of S?','2|4|6|8','4','S has perimeter 2(6 + 2) = 16 cm. T has perimeter 2(7 + 3) = 20 cm. The difference is 4 cm.','Both pairs of sides count toward a rectangle’s perimeter.',{'type':'rectangles','panels':[{'label':'S','width':6,'height':2},{'label':'T','width':7,'height':3}],'unit':'cm'}, {'kind':'numeric','expression':'2*(7+3)-2*(6+2)','visualCheck':'perimeterGap'})
visual('rectangle-area-ratio',2,'The dimensions are in centimetres. What fraction of rectangle W’s area is rectangle V’s area?','1/4|1/3|1/2|2/3','1/2','V has area 4 × 3 = 12. W has area 8 × 3 = 24. The ratio of V’s area to W’s area is 12/24 = 1/2.','With equal heights, the area ratio equals the width ratio.',{'type':'rectangles','panels':[{'label':'V','width':4,'height':3},{'label':'W','width':8,'height':3}],'unit':'cm'}, {'kind':'numeric','expression':'4*3/(8*3)','visualCheck':'areaRatio'})
visual('rectangle-square-area',2,'A square will have the same area as the rectangle shown. What must the square’s side length be, in centimetres?','4|6|8|9','6','The rectangle has area 9 × 4 = 36 square centimetres. A square of side 6 has area 6 × 6 = 36.','Match areas first, then find the square root to get a side length.',{'type':'rectangles','panels':[{'label':'Rectangle','width':9,'height':4}],'unit':'cm'}, {'kind':'numeric','expression':'6','visualCheck':'squareSide'})
visual('straight-angle-missing',1,'A, O, and B lie on a straight line. The marked angle AOC is 68°. What is angle COB, in degrees?','22|68|112|122','112','The two adjacent angles form a straight angle of 180°. Subtract 68° to get 112°.','Adjacent angles on a straight line add to 180°.',{'type':'angles','layout':'straight','known':68,'labels':['A','O','B','C']}, {'kind':'numeric','expression':'180-68','visualCheck':'straight'})
visual('right-angle-split',1,'The square mark shows that angle POQ is a right angle. Ray OR divides it, and angle POR is 34°. What is angle ROQ, in degrees?','34|46|56|146','56','A right angle is 90°. The remaining angle is 90 − 34 = 56°.','Use the right-angle mark, not an estimate of the drawing.',{'type':'angles','layout':'right','known':34,'labels':['P','O','Q','R']}, {'kind':'numeric','expression':'90-34','visualCheck':'right'})
visual('straight-three-parts',2,'A, O, and B lie on a straight line. The two marked adjacent angles are 42° and 65°. What is the remaining angle x, in degrees?','65|73|83|107','73','All three adjacent angles total 180°. The known angles total 107°, so x = 180 − 107 = 73°.','Subtract the sum of the known angles, not their difference.',{'type':'angles','layout':'three','known':[42,65],'labels':['A','O','B','C','D']}, {'kind':'numeric','expression':'180-42-65','visualCheck':'three'})
visual('right-angle-ratio',3,'The square mark shows a right angle split into the two adjacent angles labeled x and 2x. What is the larger angle, in degrees?','30|45|60|90','60','The two angles total 90°, so x + 2x = 90°. Three equal parts total 90°, making x = 30° and 2x = 60°.','Count equal parts before calculating the requested angle.',{'type':'angles','layout':'ratio','known':[1,2],'labels':['P','O','Q','R']}, {'kind':'numeric','expression':'90/3*2','visualCheck':'ratio'})
assert len(BANK)==100
